## Why This Is Asked

Tests a candidate’s ability to apply parallel bit-twiddling (SWAR) across multiple lanes in a 128-bit value, without loops, while extracting and packing results into specific lanes. It also probes attention to data layout and endianness in lane-oriented operations.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) for 32-bit lanes
- Bit masks and shifts to accumulate per-lane popcounts
- 128-bit data viewed as four 32-bit lanes and proper packing of results

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 perLanePopcount128(uint128 x) {
  auto count32 = [](uint64_t v) {
    v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    v = v + (v >> 8);
    v = v + (v >> 16);
    v = v + (v >> 32);
    return v & 0xFFULL;
  };

  uint64_t loCount  = count32(x.lo);
  uint64_t hiCount  = count32(x.hi);

  uint128 r = {0, 0};
  // lane0: lo low 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo
  r.lo = (loCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane1: lo high 32 bits -> low byte of r.lo at bits 32-39
  r.lo |= (loCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  // lane2: hi low 32 bits -> bits 64-71 of r.hi
  r.hi = (hiCount & 0xFFULL);
  // lane3: hi high 32 bits -> bits 96-103 of r.hi
  r.hi |= (hiCount & 0xFFULL) << 8;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for arbitrary lane widths (e.g., 64-bit lanes) while preserving parallelism?
- What are the potential pitfalls with endianness or portability across compilers for the approach above?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This demonstrates understanding of bit propagation tricks to solve bit constraints without loops or arithmetic, scaled to 128 bits.

## Key Concepts

- Bitwise propagation with shifts
- Boundary handling across 128 bits
- Constant-time, loop-free logic

## Code Example

```javascript
int isPowerOfTwo128(uint128 x){
  uint128 t = x;
  t |= t >> 1;
  t |= t >> 2;
  t |= t >> 4;
  t |= t >> 8;
  t |= t >> 16;
  t |= t >> 32;
  t |= t >> 64;
  return (int)(t & 1);
}
